---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="MIDI Evil — the workstation — Be Insiculous"
  description="MIDI Evil: a music workstation written in Rust — original synthesis, drum pads, a mixer with effects, .evil projects, Standard MIDI in and out — where the engine’s songs are composed."
>
  <p class="section-label">the workstation</p>
  <h1>MIDI Evil</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A music workstation written in Rust — original synthesis, drum pads, a mixer with effects, <code>.evil</code> projects, and Standard MIDI in and out — built for the engine’s songs and for its own sake.
  </p>

  <img
    class="splash"
    src="/images/ai_midi_evil_splash_320x192.png"
    alt="MIDI Evil’s title card: Dr. Maxwell, a horned red mug, between two level meters above the words MIDI EVIL"
    width="320"
    height="192"
  />

  <div class="cta">
    <a class="button" href="https://github.com/beinsiculous/midi-evil">get it on GitHub →</a>
    <a class="button secondary" href="/engine/">how songs reach the engine →</a>
  </div>

  <section>
    <h2>Instruments</h2>
    <p>
      Evil Synth is the instrument editor. Its voices start from 36 basic waveforms with
      frequency sweeps, detune, pulse width, sync and ring modes, and unison; low-, band- and
      high-pass filters; independent amplitude and filter envelopes, plus two auxiliary envelopes
      and up to eight LFO lanes, free or synced to the tempo. A waveshaper and a delay sit on its
      insert, and imported WAV and AIFF samples can be mapped across key zones. Twelve
      presets are built in, and the same patch and seed always render the same audio.
    </p>
    <p>
      Evil Beats is the drum side: a 4×4 grid of performance pads with an original drum kit,
      per-pad tuning, envelopes, trimming, reverse and gated loops, choke groups and three
      filtered output buses. A kit saves as one portable <code>.evilkit</code> file with its audio embedded, and held pads repeat at the song’s tempo.
    </p>
    <p>
      Four built-in sounds are reference-guided recreations — Max Nasty, Evil-303, GlockenBlocks
      and the Chiptune drum kit — built with original synthesis, not copied samples or factory
      audio, and calibrated against measurements of the sounds they recreate. Every synth and
      drum track then runs through the same mixer: four inserts per channel and two sends into
      shared effect buses, with EQ, compression, chorus, flanger, delay, bitcrush, reverb and a
      waveshaper.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The crossing</h2>
    <p>
      Songs cross to the engine as Standard MIDI files — <code>.mid</code>, not <code>.evil</code> — and the export keeps to the rules the engine depends on.
      Every track name is preserved exactly, because the name is how the engine picks out the
      tracks a game plays; the meter and the tempo are written at tick 0; each track’s gain and
      pan become CC 7 and CC 10, with a program change for its instrument; and a muted track
      stays in the file as an empty named track, so no track’s index shifts. The file is written
      atomically, to a temporary file in the same directory and then renamed, so the engine’s
      watcher never reads half a song.
    </p>
    <p>
      On the other side, the engine’s conductor reads the file and picks it up: background tracks
      play by themselves, and the tracks a game selects are walked one note or chord per trigger —
      a hop plays the next note of the tune. The engine’s editor opens a song in MIDI Evil with
      the right track selected, and saving writes the score back to the same file.
    </p>
    <a href="/images/midi-evil-piano-roll-2026-11.png" class="shot-link">
      <img
        src="/images/midi-evil-piano-roll-2026-11.png"
        alt="MIDI Evil’s piano roll showing Bach’s Invention No. 2 as red note blocks across four bars beside a vertical keyboard, with the track rack and its two tracks on the left, and the play and record controls, a 64 BPM tempo and a 4/4 meter along the top"
        width="1440"
        height="900"
        loading="lazy"
      />
    </a>
    <p class="caption">
      Bach’s Invention No. 2 in C minor, the public-domain example in MIDI Evil’s repository, imported and open in the piano roll. <span class="caption-hint">Tap to open it full size.</span>
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Tech stack</h2>
    <p>
      Rust throughout, in a workspace of focused crates — synthesis, drums, projects, imports,
      exports and rendering each in its own — so the preview you hear and the WAV you export come
      from one renderer. The interface is <code>eframe</code> and <code>egui</code>, audio output is <code>rodio</code>, and MIDI devices go through <code>midir</code>.
      Projects are versioned, human-readable JSON.
    </p>
    <p>
      There are more than 300 tests, and the audit that runs them is hardware-independent: no audio device,
      physical MIDI device or microphone is needed, so every change can be checked from the
      command line.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Where it runs</h2>
    <p>
      On the desktop, today: <code>cargo install --path crates/midi-evil</code> from a clone puts <code>midi-evil</code> on your <code>PATH</code>, where the engine’s editor looks for it.
      A browser build is being planned, and it ships only when it is usable with a keyboard and a
      screen reader — every editing workflow, not just the page around it.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .lede {
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    color: var(--text-bright);
    max-width: 38em;
  }

  /* Pixel art: a 2× screen doubles each pixel, and smoothing would blur them. On a phone narrower
     than 320px, global.css scales it down to the column. */
  .splash {
    image-rendering: pixelated;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  /* A wrapper, not a <p>: global.css underlines every link inside a paragraph, and these are buttons.
     The gap is on top: a bottom margin would collapse into the next heading's larger one. */
  .cta {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 1rem;
  }

  section p {
    max-width: 46em;
  }

  section img {
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
  }

  /* A 1440px screenshot of a workstation is unreadable scaled into a phone, so the image links to itself. */
  .shot-link {
    display: block;
  }

  .shot-link:hover {
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  /* Only worth saying where the image is actually too small to read. */
  .caption-hint {
    display: none;
  }

  @media (max-width: 40rem) {
    .caption-hint {
      display: inline;
    }
  }

  .caption {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--text-dim);
    margin-top: 0.5rem;
  }
</style>
